# Playwright conventions

## 1. Locators

User-facing queries, in this order: `getByRole`, then `getByLabel` or `getByPlaceholder`, then `getByText`, then `getByTestId` with a comment saying why the earlier queries cannot see the element. No CSS, no XPath, and no `page.locator()`. When several elements match, narrow with `.filter({ has })` or `.filter({ hasText })`.

✅ Good (`pages/ProgramsPage.ts`):

```typescript
this.newProgramButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: '+ New Program' });

programRow(name: string): Locator {
  return this.page.getByRole('row').filter({
    has: this.editButton(name, { exact: true }),
  });
}
```

❌ Bad (`tests/didaxis/programs.helpers.ts`):

```typescript
return row.locator('td p').first();
```

Why: a CSS path or `.first()` clicks the wrong node when the markup or the row order changes.

## 2. Waiting

Wait only with a web-first assertion that retries: `expect(locator).toBeVisible()`, `.toBeEnabled()`, or `.toHaveText()`. No `waitForTimeout`. `expect(await locator.isVisible()).toBe(true)` is a one-shot snapshot. Replacing a locator or a wait leaves the assertion's meaning as it is.

✅ Good (`tests/didaxis/ds-4-delete-program.spec.ts`):

```typescript
await expect(programs.heading).toBeVisible();
```

❌ Bad (`tests/didaxis/ds-2-edit-program.spec.ts`):

```typescript
await page.waitForTimeout(1_000);
```

Why: a fixed sleep passes while the screen is still changing, or sits idle after the screen is already ready, and a one-shot visibility check never retries.

## 3. Page Objects

One class per screen in `pages/` (`LoginPage`, `ProgramsPage`; `NewProgramModal` is the dialog that `ProgramsPage` owns). Locators are `readonly` constructor properties, methods perform actions, and every `expect()` stays in the spec. The spec does not call `getByRole`, `getByLabel`, `getByPlaceholder`, `getByText`, `getByTestId`, or `locator()`.

✅ Good (`pages/NewProgramModal.ts`):

```typescript
constructor(private readonly page: Page) {
  this.dialog = page.getByRole('dialog', { name: 'New Program' });
  this.programName = this.dialog.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Program Name' });
  this.createButton = this.dialog.getByRole('button', { name: 'Create' });
}

async submit(): Promise<void> {
  await this.createButton.click();
}
```

❌ Bad (`tests/didaxis/ds-2-edit-program.spec.ts`):

```typescript
await page.getByRole('button', { name: `Edit ${name}` }).click();
const dialog = page.getByRole('dialog', { name: 'Edit Program' });
await expect(dialog).toBeVisible();
```

Why: an inline query drifts from the page object, and an `expect()` inside a page object hides which test failed.

## 4. Auth

Stored session by default: `tests/auth.setup.ts` signs in once and saves `playwright/.auth/user.json`, and the `chromium` project depends on `setup` and loads that file. Login-page tests clear `storageState`, open `/login`, and sign in through the UI. API login is for bulk setup, one-off scripts, or a stale stored session.

✅ Good (`tests/auth.setup.ts`, `playwright.config.ts`):

```typescript
await page.context().storageState({ path: authFile });

// chromium project
use: { storageState: 'playwright/.auth/user.json' },
dependencies: ['setup'],
```

❌ Bad (a programs test logging in through the UI on every run):

```typescript
test.use({ storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] } });
await page.goto('/login');
await page.getByRole('textbox', { name: 'Email' }).fill(email);
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign In' }).click();
```

Why: a UI login in every test spends the run on the login screen and fails when that screen is slow, while a programs failure gets blamed on auth.

## 5. Test data

Import `test` from `fixtures/cleanup.fixture.ts`, build the name with `uniqueName()` (`Date.now()`), and pass `trackProgram` into `createProgram`. The worker-scoped fixture deletes those programs once through the API. No `afterAll`, and no delete through the UI. Never delete a program the test did not create. Bulk-delete may only target names with a 13-digit timestamp.

✅ Good (`tests/didaxis/ds-4-delete-program.spec.ts`, `tests/didaxis/programs.helpers.ts`):

```typescript
import { test, expect } from '../../fixtures/cleanup.fixture';

const name = uniqueName('Test Program'); // `${base}-${Date.now()}`
await programs.createProgram(name, temporaryDescription, trackProgram);
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
await programs.createProgram('Web Development 2026', 'Full-stack web development program');

test.afterAll(async ({ page }) => {
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Delete Web Development 2026' }).click();
});
```

Why: a shared name collides when `fullyParallel` is on, and a UI delete or a wide `afterAll` removes a program another test still needs.

## 6. API testing

A contract check, or data arranged without the UI, uses the Playwright `request` fixture, sends the same Bearer token the cleanup fixture uses, and asserts the status and the response shape. The call that test checks stays unmocked. `tests/api.spec.ts` is the home for those checks. A UI test still creates through `createProgram` and `trackProgram`. Worker cleanup runs after that test's `request` is gone, so `fixtures/cleanup.fixture.ts` keeps its own client.

✅ Good. Advised — `tests/api.spec.ts` is still a stub, and `tests/didaxis/programs.api.ts` calls `fetch` directly:

```typescript
test('GET /api/programs returns 200', async ({ request }) => {
  const response = await request.get('/api/programs', {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN}` },
  });
  expect(response.status()).toBe(200);
});
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
const response = await fetch(`${process.env.DIDAXIS_URL}/api/programs`);
expect(response.ok).toBe(true);
```

Why: a raw `fetch` in a spec skips `baseURL` and the test trace, so a contract failure looks like a network accident rather than a wrong status or body.

## 7. Network mocking

Deterministic UI edge cases fulfill `/api/programs` with `page.route`. Mock only the response whose effect on the UI is under test. Never mock the endpoint that test verifies. One test per case, tagged `@edge`. Assert the UI, not the status just fulfilled. Read the error and empty-state copy on the live Programs page before writing the assertion. If the case renders nothing, say so.

✅ Good. Advised — no spec calls `page.route` yet:

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/programs', async (route) => {
  if (route.request().method() !== 'POST') {
    await route.continue();
    return;
  }
  await route.fulfill({ status: 503, contentType: 'application/json', body: '{}' });
});

const programs = new ProgramsPage(page);
await programs.goto();
await programs.openNewProgramForm();
await programs.newProgramModal.fill(name, description);
await programs.newProgramModal.submit();
await expect(programs.newProgramModal.dialog).toBeVisible();
```

The `toBeVisible()` line stands in until the live page has been read. Replace it with the error copy that page actually shows.

❌ Bad:

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/**', (route) => route.abort());
await page.waitForTimeout(5_000);
```

Why: a blanket abort, including the call the test is supposed to verify, turns a real API failure into a fake one, and a sleep never proves the UI reached its error state.

### Programs API edge cases

Save means `POST /api/programs` for create and `PUT` or `PATCH /api/programs/*` for edit. List means `GET /api/programs`. Real create, list, edit, delete, duplicate-name validation, `tests/api.spec.ts`, and cleanup `DELETE /api/programs/<uuid>` stay on the network. `page.route` stays in the spec. Clicks, fills, and navigation go through `ProgramsPage`, `NewProgramModal`, and `LoginPage`.

- 503 on save → the UI's error state
- 200 and `[]` on list → the empty-state message
- 200 with a malformed payload (`{`, `null`, or `{ "programs": null }`) on the call the screen reads → the app does not crash
- 400, 408, 422, and 429 on save → the UI's error state
- 409 on save → the UI's conflict state. This is not the real duplicate-name test
- 401 and 403 on the call the screen makes → the UI's auth or forbidden state
- 404 on list → the UI's missing-list state
- 500, 501, 502, and 504 on the call the screen makes → the UI's error state
- the call never completes (`route.abort('timedout')`) → the UI's timeout or error state
- 300 on that call, and 204 or `{}` on list → no crash, or the empty state, whichever the UI actually does

## 8. Accessibility

A spec that opens a screen follows `.agents/skills/a11y-checks/SKILL.md`. The programs page has two tests, each with exactly one tag, `@regression`: an axe scan with `withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])` asserted by `expect(results.violations).toEqual([])`, and a keyboard check that tabs to the primary control, asserts `toBeFocused()`, presses Enter, and asserts the dialog is visible. On Programs that control is `ProgramsPage.newProgramButton` and the dialog is `newProgramModal.dialog`. Call `.include()` or `.exclude()` only for a noisy third-party widget, with a comment that says why. If `violations` is not empty, report rule id, impact, help text, and targets, then stop.

✅ Good. Advised — no spec passes `withTags` or presses Tab. This is the scan from `.agents/skills/a11y-checks/SKILL.md`:

```typescript
await expect(programs.heading).toBeVisible();
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
  .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])
  .analyze();
await expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);

await expect(programs.newProgramButton).toBeFocused();
await page.keyboard.press('Enter');
await expect(programs.newProgramModal.dialog).toBeVisible();
```

❌ Bad (`tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts`):

```typescript
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page }).include('[role="dialog"]').analyze();
await expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
```

Why: a scan without the WCAG tags misses the level the project promised to hold, and scoping the scan to the dialog hides a violation on the rest of the page. Axe also never proves the primary control is reachable from the keyboard.

## 9. Assertions

Assertions are web-first and retry. `expect.soft` is for one test that checks several independent facts, so the first miss does not hide the rest. A test of one behavior uses a hard `expect`. `toHaveScreenshot` is for a layout that role and text queries cannot see. Copy that `toHaveText` can check stays a text assertion. Snapshots run under the Runner locale and timezone.

✅ Good (`tests/didaxis/ds-4-delete-program.spec.ts`):

```typescript
await expect(programs.programRow(name)).toBeVisible();
await expect(programs.programTitle(name)).toHaveText(name);
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
expect(await programs.heading.isVisible()).toBe(true);
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot();
```

Why: a one-shot check fails once and never retries, a screenshot of copy that `toHaveText` already covers flakes on font and locale, and a hard `expect` stops the test before the other independent facts report.

Advised — nothing in the repo calls `expect.soft` or `toHaveScreenshot`:

```typescript
await expect.soft(programs.programTitle(name)).toHaveText(name);
await expect.soft(programs.programDescription(name, description)).toHaveText(description);

await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('programs-layout.png');
```

Use the soft pair only when those two facts are independent. Use the screenshot only when the risk is layout.

## 10. Anti-flake

Each test passes when run alone. It creates its own data with `uniqueName()` and leaves other tests' records untouched. CI retries twice (`process.env.CI ? 2 : 0`), local runs do not retry, and `retries` stays at most 2. Do not set `workers`, and do not add `test.describe.serial` to hide a collision. `use.locale` is `'en-CA'` and `use.timezoneId` is `'America/Toronto'`. A relative timestamp (`just now`, `5 minutes ago`) freezes time with `page.clock.install()` before `goto()`.

✅ Good (`playwright.config.ts`):

```typescript
fullyParallel: true,
retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0,
use: {
  baseURL: process.env.DIDAXIS_URL,
  locale: 'en-CA',
  timezoneId: 'America/Toronto',
  trace: 'on-first-retry',
},
```

❌ Bad (`tests/didaxis/ds-4-delete-program.spec.ts`, and a config that serializes the run):

```typescript
test.describe.configure({ mode: 'serial' });

// playwright.config.ts
retries: 2,
workers: 1,
```

Why: local retries hide the first failure, `workers: 1` or `serial` hides a test that only passes because another test ran first, and an unfrozen "just now" differs between a laptop and CI.

### Runner

The orchestrator edits `playwright.config.ts` to match this section. test-writer does not edit that file. Leave `testIgnore`, `forbidOnly`, the dotenv load, and the reporter as they are.

- `fullyParallel: true`
- `retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0`. Never set `retries` above 2.
- Do not set `workers`. Remove `workers: process.env.CI ? 1 : undefined` if it is present. Do not set `workers: 1`.
- `use.baseURL` is `process.env.DIDAXIS_URL`. Do not hardcode the site URL.
- `use.trace` is `'on-first-retry'`.
- `use.locale` is `'en-CA'` and `use.timezoneId` is `'America/Toronto'`.
- Keep the `setup` project (`testMatch: /.*\.setup\.ts/`) and the `chromium` project that depends on `setup` and sets `storageState: 'playwright/.auth/user.json'`.

### Frozen clock

A spec that asserts a relative timestamp freezes time with `page.clock.install()` before `goto()`. The expected text is the relative phrase the live UI renders at that frozen instant. The locator is a page-object method. The assertion stays in the spec. The programs list is fulfilled with `page.route`, and `created_at` is that same frozen instant. `trackProgram` comes from the fixtures and is not called, because no program is created.

Advised — no spec calls `page.clock.install()` yet:

```typescript
test('program row shows a relative timestamp from a frozen clock', async ({ page, trackProgram }) => {
  const name = uniqueName('Test Program');
  const createdAt = '2026-03-15T12:00:00.000Z';

  await page.clock.install({ time: new Date(createdAt) });
  await page.route('**/api/programs', async (route) => {
    const pathname = new URL(route.request().url()).pathname.replace(/\/$/, '');
    if (route.request().method() !== 'GET' || pathname !== '/api/programs') {
      await route.continue();
      return;
    }
    await route.fulfill({
      status: 200,
      contentType: 'application/json',
      body: JSON.stringify({
        data: [{ id: '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', name, created_at: createdAt }],
      }),
    });
  });

  // The fulfilled list creates nothing, so trackProgram is not called.
  void trackProgram;

  const programs = new ProgramsPage(page);
  await programs.goto();

  // Placeholder until the relative phrase is read from the live page at this frozen time.
  await expect(programs.programRow(name)).toContainText('<relative timestamp>');
});
```

## Do / Don't

| | Do | Don't |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Locators | `getByRole`, then label, placeholder, or text. `getByTestId` only with a comment. Narrow with `.filter()`. | CSS, XPath, `page.locator()`, `.first()`. |
| Waiting | `expect(locator).toBeVisible()` / `toBeEnabled()` / `toHaveText()`. | `waitForTimeout`. `expect(await locator.isVisible()).toBe(true)`. |
| Page objects | Locators in the constructor. Actions in methods. `ProgramsPage` owns the modal. | `expect()` in a page object. Inline queries in the spec. |
| Auth | `storageState` from the setup project. API login for bulk setup, scripts, or a stale session. | UI login in every programs test. |
| Test data | `uniqueName()` and `trackProgram` from `fixtures/cleanup.fixture.ts`. | A shared name, `afterAll`, a UI delete, or deleting someone else's program. |
| API testing | The `request` fixture for contract checks and API setup. | Raw `fetch` in a spec. Mocking the call that test checks. |
| Network mocking | `page.route` + `fulfill` for 500, 503, timeout, empty, and malformed. One `@edge` test. Copy from the live page. | Aborting `**/api/**`. Inventing the error string. |
| Accessibility | `withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])` and a Tab check to `newProgramButton`. One `@regression` tag. | `disableRules()`. `.include()` / `.exclude()` on the app's own UI. |
| Assertions | Web-first `expect`. `expect.soft` for independent facts. `toHaveScreenshot` for layout. | A one-shot snapshot. A screenshot of text `toHaveText` can check. |
| Anti-flake | CI-only retries (max 2). `en-CA` / `America/Toronto`. `page.clock` before `goto()` for relative time. Each test passes alone. | `workers: 1`. `test.describe.serial` to hide a collision. Local retries. An unfrozen "just now". |
